# Build context exclusions.
#
# FIX (2026-08-14): there was no .dockerignore at all, so `docker build` on the
# validator box shipped the ENTIRE working directory as build context — 1.22 GB,
# measured in the deploy log. Breakdown at the time:
#
#     .git           1079 MB
#     node_modules    365 MB
#     .venv           176 MB
#     aequitasd.exe    51 MB
#
# None of it is used by the Dockerfile. Transferring it cost ~107 s before the
# build even started, and that overhead was a direct contributor to the deploy
# exceeding its 600 s command timeout — which killed the deploy AFTER the old
# container had already been stopped, leaving Contabo1 down.
#
# Keep in mind what the Dockerfile actually needs from the context:
#   go.mod / go.sum, the Go sources, genesis.json, aequitas-dapp.html, downloads/
# Everything else can go.

# Version control — by far the largest single item.
.git
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github

# Dependency trees for the OTHER toolchains (Hardhat/TS, Python). The Go build
# needs neither.
node_modules
.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc

# Prebuilt binaries and release artifacts. The image builds its own.
*.exe
*.apk
aequitasd

# Hardhat / Solidity build output — compiled inside CI, never by this image.
artifacts
cache
ignition
contracts
test
scripts
typechain
types
coverage

# Local tooling and scratch space.
.claude
.agents
build
*.log
.job_log.txt

# Screenshots and other stray media committed at the repo root.
screen_check*.png

# Docs — not needed to compile a binary.
*.md
docs
deploy

# Local env files must never enter an image layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ...except the build-commit stamp, which the deploy workflows write into the
# checkout right before `docker build` so the image can record which commit it
# was built from. Kept explicit: this file is small, required, and easy to
# exclude by accident with a broader dot-file rule later.
!.git-commit-stamp
